course: dictionary
lesson: 5373
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word knacker is a good example. «This clock's knackered!» means the clock is broken or worn out, while «I'm knackered, love, sorry» means someone is very tired. Read the words around it to choose the right meaning.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. «Sent to the knacker's yard» is an old-fashioned British phrase. Here the clue is "sent", and the meaning is serious: an old horse was sent away to be killed and used for meat or glue. It is not about anyone being tired.
explain: Some meanings are rude or hurtful. Knacker can be an insulting word for a rough person, and it hurts people, especially Irish Travellers, so never use it to call anyone names. There is also a rude slang meaning that is not for school. If you meet it in a text, ask a grown-up.
fact: Knacker is informal slang in Britain, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: To knacker something means to break it or wear it out.
fact: To knacker someone means to make them very tired, as in «I'm knackered».
fact: A knacker is an old-fashioned word for a person whose job is buying old or dead horses and turning them into meat or glue.
fact: Calling someone a knacker as an insult is rude and hurtful.
exercise: What does «This clock's knackered!» mean? | Very loud when it ticks | Brand new and shiny | Very early in the morning | *Broken or worn out
exercise: «Sent to the knacker's yard» was an old-fashioned phrase. Where was the old horse sent? | To a race with a big prize | To a farm for a long rest in the sun | To school for lessons | *To be turned into meat or glue
exercise: Mia's dad says «I'm knackered, love, sorry». What does knackered mean here? | Very angry | Very hungry | Very cold | *Very tired
test: «Ready for the knacker's yard» is a joke about something. What does it mean? | Fully fixed and brand new | Ready for a big party | Ready to go swimming | *Too old or worn out to be useful
test: Why should you be careful with the word knacker when it means a rough person? | It means a small wee dog | *It is a rude, hurtful insult | It means something very kind | It is the name of a famous singer
exam: Read «The old van is knackered after years on the road.» What does knackered mean? | Painted bright red | Clean and shiny | Full of petrol | *Broken or worn out after years of use
